  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n\nThe optimized strategy breaks symmetry and implements a two-phase approach to escape local minima and explore the solution space more thoroughly. By combining simulated annealing with deterministic solvers and carefully designed perturbations, we aim to find a better minimal overlap.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n\nVariables:\n\n- h \u2208 [0, 1]^(n_points)\n- \u2211 h = n_points / 2\n- Objective: min max_{k} \u2211 h[i] (1 - h[i+k mod n_points]) dx\n\nFormulation:\n\nThe problem is non-convex due to the product h[i](1 - h[i+k]). We model it directly, using a mixed-integer search strategy.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\nPhase 1: Simulated Annealing (10 minutes)\na. Initialize h using initial_h_values, breaking symmetry slightly\nb. Randomly perturb h, repairing constraints\nc. Compute C5; accept worse solutions with decreasing probability\n\nPhase 2: Deterministic Refinement (remaining time)\na. Use SLSQP to minimize C5 from the best candidate of Phase 1\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n\n- Perturb entire h instead of symmetric halves\n- Vary perturbation magnitude adapting to progress\n- Alternate perturbation sizes to explore different scales\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n\nSimulated Annealing: ~1000 evaluations\nSLSQP: ~200 iterations\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n\nEnsure \u2211 h = n_points / 2 and 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1. Return C5 \u2264 0.38085",
